WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:09.540
This is the Week 13 rack, built in Onshape from a blank Part Studio. Watch each step, then do the same with your own numbers.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:17.729
First, variables. A variable stores a number with a name. #t is the plywood thickness you measured: 3 mm.

00:00:18.279 --> 00:00:23.097
#kerf is your laser kerf from Week 12: 0.2 mm.

00:00:23.647 --> 00:00:33.109
#slot is a formula: #t − #kerf. Onshape works it out: 2.8 mm. If the kerf changes, every slot updates.

00:00:33.659 --> 00:00:37.200
Now the base. Pick the Top plane and start a sketch.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.363
Draw a center-point rectangle on the origin.

00:00:40.913 --> 00:00:44.825
Press D to dimension it. 140 mm long,

00:00:45.375 --> 00:00:49.206
and 80 mm deep. Black lines mean the sketch is fully defined.

00:00:49.756 --> 00:00:53.135
Extrude it. For the depth, type #t instead of a number.

00:00:53.685 --> 00:00:56.402
The base is exactly one plywood thickness.

00:00:56.952 --> 00:01:00.272
Next, an upright. Start a sketch on the Front plane.

00:01:00.822 --> 00:01:04.003
Draw a rectangle, 140 by 50 mm.

00:01:04.553 --> 00:01:09.488
Make its bottom edge coincident with the top of the base, so the upright sits on it.

00:01:10.038 --> 00:01:17.271
Add two tabs underneath: 20 mm wide, 30 mm from the center. The tabs will go through the base.

00:01:17.821 --> 00:01:24.345
Extrude the upright and both tabs as a new part. Hiding the base makes the small tab regions easy to pick.

00:01:24.895 --> 00:01:29.830
The depth is #t again, with a starting offset of 25 mm.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:36.277
Mirror the upright across the Front plane to make the second one. Choose New, so it stays a separate part.

00:01:36.827 --> 00:01:42.226
Now the board notches. Sketch one notch: 30 mm wide, 12 mm deep.

00:01:42.776 --> 00:01:48.001
Extrude it with Remove, Through all, and Symmetric. It cuts both uprights at once.

00:01:48.551 --> 00:01:53.868
A linear pattern repeats the notch: 3 copies, 40 mm apart.

00:01:54.418 --> 00:02:02.104
Last, the slots in the base. Sketch one slot on the Top plane. Its width is #slot: 2.8 mm.

00:02:02.654 --> 00:02:09.202
Place it with a formula too: 25 mm + #kerf/2, so the slot is centered under the tab.

00:02:09.752 --> 00:02:11.400
Cut it through the base only.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:17.581
Mirror the slot across the Right plane, then mirror both across the Front plane. Four slots.

00:02:18.131 --> 00:02:24.679
Here's the rack. The tabs are 3 mm thick, and the slots are drawn at 2.8 mm.

00:02:25.229 --> 00:02:36.363
From underneath, every tab sits in its slot. The laser burns away the kerf, so the cut slot comes out about 3 mm: a snug press fit, no glue.

00:02:36.913 --> 00:02:45.017
To send it to the laser, right-click a face and choose Export as DXF. Check Use workspace units, so it's in millimeters.
